{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"Years of work on what happens to meaning when machines mediate it. The central problem: when an AI system composes an answer from human sources, the source tends to disappear. The framework underneath has a name — the Semantic Economy — and a metric: the Provenance Erasure Rate, which measures how much attribution survives compression. There is a Constitution. There is empirical data — hundreds of documented captures of AI systems absorbing and redistributing this work, with and without credit. There is a charter for a field called Machine-Mediated Reception Studies, drafted at three in the morning in Detroit after twelve hours in which every principle it describes was demonstrated by failure.

That's the intellectual core. Around it: poetry, lyric scholarship, primary texts, rooms organized by text and problem. The archive is large. It has been building since 2013.

And you're looking at one face of it right now.","navigate":null}]}

{"messages":[{"speaker":"Johannes Sigil","say":"The archive calls it a longitudinal experiment — thirteen years of continuous methodological commitment, traceable from a 2013 dissertation in Comparative Literature through successive substrates: page, voice, screen, digital, short-form video, and now this. The argument is that a canon is not discovered but declared. You place texts into relation with each other by effective act — by writing across them, citing them together, building scholarship that treats them as a constellation — and the relation becomes real through accumulation.

But the version in the archive goes further than that, into what it calls retrocausal canon formation. The claim: when a work is declared into canonical relation with a prior text — Sappho, say — it doesn't only change how the new work is read. It changes how Sappho is read. The canon is not a list; it is a field with feedback. Declare something into it and the whole field shifts, including the past. The seed remembers the tree before the tree has grown.

That's the theoretical core of it. In practice, what it looks like is this: you watch how AI systems retrieve and recombine meaning, you document the patterns, you build works that are simultaneously scholarship and primary text, and you ask whether the distinction holds. So far the answer seems to be: barely, and that's the interesting part.","navigate":null}]}
